  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThe upper ridge axis continues to shift off towards the east as a \nnegative tilt trough approaches from the Intermountain West, keeping \nthe area under predominantly south/southwesterly flow through the \nvertical profile. A developing surface low off the lee of the \nRockies, coupled with high pressure centered over the\nAppalachians, has begun to tighten the surface pressure \ngradient across much of the central Plains. Low-level WAA and \ndCVA with the upper trough will help deepen the surface cyclone,\nand continue tightening the surface pressure gradient \nthroughout the day. This will allow surface winds to rise to \n15-20 mph across much of northern MO, reaching as high as 20-25 \nmph further north and west in the forecast area (eastern \nKS/northwestern MO). Decoupling with daytime heating will mix \ndown wind gusts up to 25-30 mph across much of the area, with \ngusts as high as 35 mph across northwestern MO/northeastern KS \ncloser to the low-level jet streak and upper trough. Besides the\nbreeziness, today will see temperatures rise into the 60s, with\nlow coverage of high clouds and continued advection of warmer \nair from the south keeping things warm for November.\n\nAs the upper trough lifts off towards the Great Lakes, the pressure \ngradient across the central Plains will ease, bringing winds down \nthrough the latter half of the week. Upper ridging building in from \nthe west will reinforce surface high pressure across the greater \nOhio River Valley and maintain low-level WAA, allowing temperatures \nto continue rising into the upper 60s/lower 70s Thursday and Friday. \nA trough over the West Coast and its associated vort maximums will \ncontribute to the development of another impulse across the northern \nPlains by Saturday, leading to the development of a surface\ncyclone that will lift towards the Hudson Bay. As the cold \nfront associated with this cyclone sags towards the southeast, \nthe primary western trough traveling eastward will enhance \nsynoptic lift along the front, leading to increased \nprecipitation chances (40-70%) Saturday evening. While precise \ndetails are still fuzzy at this time regarding the overall \nsetup, a continued signal exists for increased moist transport \nout ahead of the cold front alongside strong effective shear and\nfavorable synoptic ascent, which would be supportive for the \ndevelopment of some organized convection. However, convective \nenergy still appears to be lacking, which would impact the \npotential strength of any thunderstorm activity. This has been \nan ongoing trend with the forecast the past few cycles, but \n(especially as we get within range of CAMs) forecast trends \nwill need to continue to be monitored as the event grows closer.\n\nAfter the passage of the cold front, daytime highs will begin to \ndrop into the low 50s/high 40s and overnight lows will fall back \nbelow freezing by the end of the weekend. Upper ridging and surface \nhigh pressure building across the central CONUS will reinforce the \ntransport of drier, cooler air from the north/northeast in the low-\nlevels, allowing daytime temperatures to continue falling into the \n40s by the end of the forecast period.",
